Future<void> shutdownPty(
 PseudoTerminal pty, {
 bool hard = false,
 Duration? grace,
 double? budgetScale,
}) async {
 final scale = budgetScale ?? resolvePtyWaitBudgetScale(envValue: Platform.environment[kPtyWaitBudgetScaleEnv]);
 final soft = grace ?? const Duration(seconds: 5);
 final softBudget = scalePtyWaitBudget(soft, scale);
 final killBudget = scalePtyWaitBudget(const Duration(seconds: 5), scale);
 if (hard) {
   pty.kill(ProcessSignal.sigkill);
 } else {
   pty.kill(); // SIGTERM + SIGHUP (pty2 pairs them for the read-loop unblock)
 }
 if (await _exitWithin(pty, softBudget)) return;
 // Escalate: the child ignored SIGTERM (a `dart run` dartdev teardown or
 // a signal-trapping child). pty2's kill() is once-only, so re-send
 // through Process.killPid — this is why the vendored fork exposes pid.
 Process.killPid(pty.pid, ProcessSignal.sigkill);
 if (await _exitWithin(pty, killBudget)) return;
 throw StateError(...);
}

Future<bool> _exitWithin(PseudoTerminal pty, Duration budget) async {
 try {
   await pty.exitCode.timeout(budget);
   return true;
 } on TimeoutException {
   return false;
 }
``

Wait — one subtlety: `pty.kill(ProcessSignal.sigkill)` when `hard=true` — pty2's kill sends SIGKILL AND ALSO SIGHUP (the second kill(_pid,1) in pty2's kill runs unconditionally after the mapped signal — look: `final ret = unix.kill(_pid, sigNum) == 0; unix.kill(_pid, 1); _closePtm();` — yes, SIGHUP always follows). Fine.

Another subtlety: after `pty.kill()`, pty2's outer `_ptm = -1` (write no-ops). Good.

exitCode future: pty2's `_exitCodeFuture = exitPort.first...` — `.timeout(budget)` does NOT cancel the underlying wait — safe to re-await the SAME future after escalation (a second `.timeout()` on the same future — allowed).

BUT — if the FIRST `.timeout(budget)` expires, the pty.exitCode future is left pending; after SIGKILL it completes; the second await picks it up. Good. HOWEVER: an abandoned `.timeout()` leaves a Timer that fires later — after we've returned — completing an already-completed... the timeout timer fires with TimeoutException delivered to an abandoned future — no unhandled error (the wrapper future has no listener after we stopped awaiting → hmm, actually `future.timeout(budget)` returns a NEW future; if we don't await it after it errors → **unhandled async error!** `await pty.exitCode.timeout(...)` inside `_exitWithin`: we DO await it — the await throws TimeoutException → we catch it. The underlying `pty.exitCode` completes later with a value — no listener → fine (a VALUE with no listener is not an error). So no unhandled errors. 

One more: for `hard=true` when the child ALREADY ignores SIGKILL (impossible — SIGKILL can't be trapped) → the only hang risk is D-state (nfs) — the throw covers it.

The dartdev edge: `dart run` — SIGKILL kills dartdev instantly. Its INNER child (the actual example VM) becomes an orphan holding the pty slave — `_readUntilExit`'s poll loop: `PtyCoreUnix._isProcessDead(pid)` (direct child dead) → read() returns null → free() closes master → isolate exits. waitpid returned → `_waitForExitCode` exits. NO WEDGE. Grandchildren orphans are harmless (reparented to init; the runner's exit doesn't wait on them — the VM only waits for its OWN isolates).

